<h1 style="font-size:26px;margin:0 0 4px 0;text-align:left;">Knowledge Article: MECM Client Policy Reset and Client Reinstall</h1>
<p style="color:#555555;margin-top:0;">Audience: Local Administrators &middot; Last updated: 13 November 2026</p>

<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:32px 0 8px 0;padding-bottom:4px;border-bottom:1px solid #cccccc;text-align:left;">Purpose</h2>
<p>This article gives Local Administrators two ways to recover a device whose MECM (Configuration Manager) client is not receiving software updates or not communicating with the site.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Option 1, reset client policy:</strong> run the script in this article. It takes about 7 minutes, needs no restart and is safe to run on a healthy client. Try this first.</li>
  <li><strong>Option 2, reinstall the client:</strong> use this only if Option 1 does not fix the device.</li>
</ul>
<p>Background: following the MECM policy signing incident in October and November 2026, some clients are holding out-of-date policy locally. The client does not clear this on its own, so it has to be reset.</p>

<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:32px 0 8px 0;padding-bottom:4px;border-bottom:1px solid #cccccc;text-align:left;">When to use this article</h2>
<p>Use it when a device shows one or more of these symptoms:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Software updates are not appearing, or not installing, in Software Center.</li>
  <li>Applications or updates sit at "waiting" or 0% and never download.</li>
  <li>Software Center is empty or missing items it normally shows.</li>
  <li>The Configuration Manager control panel Actions tab lists only one or two actions.</li>
  <li>The device shows as inactive in the MECM console, or has no recent policy request or heartbeat.</li>
  <li><code style="font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace;background:#f2f2f2;padding:1px 4px;">C:\Windows\CCM\Logs\PolicyAgent.log</code> shows errors about a policy signature or hash failing verification.</li>
</ul>

<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:32px 0 8px 0;padding-bottom:4px;border-bottom:1px solid #cccccc;text-align:left;">Before you start</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rights:</strong> you need local administrator rights on the device.</li>
  <li><strong>Network:</strong> the device must be on the network and able to reach the MECM management point.</li>
  <li><strong>Time:</strong> the script runs for about 7 minutes. Do not close the window while it runs.</li>
  <li><strong>Impact:</strong> no restart is needed and the user can keep working. The client downloads all of its policy again, so deployments can take 30 to 60 minutes to reappear in Software Center.</li>
  <li><strong>Record:</strong> note the device name (run <code style="font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace;background:#f2f2f2;padding:1px 4px;">hostname</code>) in case you need to escalate.</li>
</ul>

<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:32px 0 8px 0;padding-bottom:4px;border-bottom:1px solid #cccccc;text-align:left;">Option 1: Reset client policy</h2>
<p>The script clears the client's local policy, requests a fresh copy from the site, then starts a software update scan and evaluation.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Sign in to the device with a local administrator account.</li>
  <li>Open the Start menu, type <strong>PowerShell</strong>, right-click <strong>Windows PowerShell</strong> and choose <strong>Run as administrator</strong>.</li>
  <li>Copy the whole script below, paste it into the window and press Enter. If it stops at the last line, press Enter once more.</li>
  <li>Wait about 7 minutes. The window shows nothing until the script finishes.</li>
  <li>Read the single line of output, then go to "Check the result".</li>
</ol>
<p>Paste the script directly into the window. A saved <code style="font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace;background:#f2f2f2;padding:1px 4px;">.ps1</code> file may be blocked by the device's script execution policy.</p>

<pre style="font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace;font-size:13px;background:#f2f2f2;border:1px solid #cccccc;padding:12px;margin:12px 0;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto;text-align:left;"><code style="font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace;"># Runs one client action and records whether it succeeded
function Invoke-ClientAction {
    param(
        [string]$Name,
        [string]$Method,
        [hashtable]$Arguments
    )

    try {
        $r = Invoke-CimMethod -Namespace 'root\ccm' -ClassName 'SMS_Client' `
                              -MethodName $Method -Arguments $Arguments -ErrorAction Stop

        if ($null -ne $r.ReturnValue -and $r.ReturnValue -ne 0) {
            return "$Name=FAILED (return $($r.ReturnValue))"
        }
        return "$Name=OK"
    }
    catch {
        return "$Name=FAILED ($($_.Exception.Message.Trim()))"
    }
}

$results = @()

# 1. Purge local policy
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Reset' -Method 'ResetPolicy' -Arguments @{ uFlags = [uint32]1 }
Start-Sleep -Seconds 60

# 2. Machine policy retrieval
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Policy' -Method 'TriggerSchedule' -Arguments @{ sScheduleID = '{00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021}' }
Start-Sleep -Seconds 180

# 3. Software updates scan
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Scan' -Method 'TriggerSchedule' -Arguments @{ sScheduleID = '{00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000113}' }
Start-Sleep -Seconds 120

# 4. Software updates deployment evaluation
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Eval' -Method 'TriggerSchedule' -Arguments @{ sScheduleID = '{00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108}' }

# One line of output per client
$results -join '; '</code></pre>

